Anything else the user points `ct2_model_dir` / `translate.model` at is the
user's own choice, under that model's own licence.


Permissive alternatives to NLLB
-------------------------------

These are permissively licensed, and both are a config change rather than a
code change — the `local` backend loads any HuggingFace seq2seq model and
`ct2` loads any converted one:

  facebook/m2m100_418M              MIT
  Helsinki-NLP/opus-mt-en-jap       Apache-2.0

Two caveats, both real:

  * A different model family expects its own language codes. LinTranslator's language
    picker offers FLORES-200 codes (`eng_Latn`, `jpn_Jpan`) because that is what
    NLLB was trained on; m2m100 wants `en` / `ja`. Until the language table
    carries per-model codes, swapping the model means also handling that.
  * Quality is not equal. This project measured `opus-mt-en-jap` producing
    output unrelated to its input on its own reference line (see README,
    "NLLB, never opus-mt-en-jap"), and NLLB-600M was chosen over it on measured
    quality.

For commercial use without any of this, use a hosted backend (OpenRouter,
OpenAI, DeepL, or your own OpenAI-compatible endpoint): then no local model is
involved and the provider's own terms apply.


